const passages = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"passageNumber\":1,\"title\":\"Agricultural Robotics and the Rise of Precision Farming\",\"subtitle\":\"Autonomous swarms, hyperspectral vision, and micro-dosing are transforming commercial agriculture\",\"wordCount\":810,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nFor the past century, commercial agriculture has pursued a relentless paradigm of gigantism. Heavy diesel tractors weighing upwards of twenty metric tons, colossal combine harvesters, and wide-boom chemical sprayers transformed farming into an industrial process capable of feeding billions. Yet this model has incurred catastrophic environmental and economic costs: massive machines compact delicate topsoil into impermeable layers that stunt root penetration and exacerbate flood runoff, while broadcast pesticide spraying wastes up to ninety percent of applied chemicals onto non-target weeds and bare soil. Over the past decade, however, an engineering revolution combining artificial intelligence, computer vision, and autonomous swarms has birthed precision agricultural robotics—a paradigm that replaces gigantic machinery with agile, intelligent micro-machines.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nAt the core of modern agricultural robotics is multispectral and hyperspectral computer vision. Unlike standard RGB optical sensors that replicate human sight, hyperspectral cameras capture hundreds of narrow electromagnetic bands across the visible, near-infrared, and shortwave-infrared spectra. By measuring the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) and specific chlorophyll fluorescence signatures, autonomous field robots can diagnose plant distress—such as nitrogen deficiency, fungal blight, or parasitic mite infestations—days before chlorotic yellowing or leaf curling becomes discernible to a human agronomist.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nArmed with this hyper-acute diagnostic capability, autonomous robotic weeders operate with surgical accuracy. Traditional broadcast spraying douses entire hectarage in broad-spectrum synthetic herbicides. In contrast, robots like the Swiss-developed ecoRobotix or the American LaserWeeder travel along crop rows utilizing edge-computing neural networks to classify every individual seedling in milliseconds. Once an invasive weed is identified among commercial seedlings, the machine executes targeted micro-dosing: firing micro-droplets of concentrated organic herbicide directly onto the weed's apical bud, or neutralizing it instantly with high-energy carbon dioxide laser pulses. These robotic systems reduce chemical herbicide usage by up to ninety-five percent while completely eliminating soil toxicity and chemical drift.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nBeyond chemical suppression, autonomous robots are tackling the global agricultural labor crisis. Harvesting delicate soft fruits, such as strawberries, raspberries, and table grapes, has historically resisted mechanization because human pickers rely on nuanced tactile feedback to pluck ripe fruit without bruising tender skin. Contemporary harvesting robots overcome this hurdle through compliant soft-robotic pneumatic grippers fabricated from silicone elastomers. Guided by stereoscopic depth sensors that compute the exact three-dimensional Cartesian coordinates and stem angles of each fruit, robotic arms delicately grasp and twist fruit with calibrated pressure that mimics human touch, harvesting day and night without fatigue.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nDespite their evident technological potential, agricultural robotics faces socio-technical headwinds before achieving ubiquitous adoption. High capital acquisition expenses, which can reach several hundred thousand dollars per unit, deter smallholder farmers who lack access to venture capital or favorable agricultural loans. Furthermore, agricultural fields present harsh, non-standardized outdoor environments characterized by heavy dust, driving rain, mud, and fluctuating solar illumination that can blind optical sensors and impede wheel traction. Nevertheless, forward-looking agronomists forecast that 'Robotics-as-a-Service' (RaaS) subscription models, wherein third-party robotic operators harvest and weed fields on an hourly fee basis, will democratize access across diverse agricultural scales.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1–6 on your answer sheet, write:\\nTRUE if the statement agrees with the information\\nFALSE if the statement contradicts the information\\nNOT GIVEN if there is no information on this\",\"questionType\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q1\",\"questionNumber\":1,\"prompt\":\"Heavier tractors have contributed to soil compaction that restricts crop root growth.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A explicitly notes that heavy machines 'compact delicate topsoil into impermeable layers that stunt root penetration'.\",\"evidence\":\"massive machines compact delicate topsoil into impermeable layers that stunt root penetration and exacerbate flood runoff\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q2\",\"questionNumber\":2,\"prompt\":\"Hyperspectral cameras can identify plant diseases before visible leaf yellowing appears.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains hyperspectral cameras can diagnose distress 'days before chlorotic yellowing or leaf curling becomes discernible to a human agronomist.'\",\"evidence\":\"autonomous field robots can diagnose plant distress—such as nitrogen deficiency, fungal blight, or parasitic mite infestations—days before chlorotic yellowing or leaf curling becomes discernible to a human agronomist.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q3\",\"questionNumber\":3,\"prompt\":\"Robotic weeders are only capable of operating during sunny daylight hours.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C and D describe robots operating with edge computing and lighting, and Paragraph D states harvesting robots harvest 'day and night without fatigue.'\",\"evidence\":\"harvesting day and night without fatigue.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q4\",\"questionNumber\":4,\"prompt\":\"Soft robotic grippers for fruit harvesting are constructed using silicone elastomers.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D mentions 'compliant soft-robotic pneumatic grippers fabricated from silicone elastomers.'\",\"evidence\":\"Contemporary harvesting robots overcome this hurdle through compliant soft-robotic pneumatic grippers fabricated from silicone elastomers.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q5\",\"questionNumber\":5,\"prompt\":\"Agricultural robots in Europe must pass mandatory governmental safety inspections every year.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"The passage discusses European and American robots, but mentions no mandatory annual governmental safety inspections.\",\"evidence\":\"In contrast, robots like the Swiss-developed ecoRobotix or the American LaserWeeder travel along crop rows\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q6\",\"questionNumber\":6,\"prompt\":\"The Robotics-as-a-Service model requires smallholder farmers to purchase the machines outright.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E explains that RaaS is a subscription model where 'third-party robotic operators harvest and weed fields on an hourly fee basis', rather than farmers buying machines outright.\",\"evidence\":\"wherein third-party robotic operators harvest and weed fields on an hourly fee basis, will democratize access across diverse agricultural scales.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 7–13 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q7\",\"questionNumber\":7,\"prompt\":\"Traditional wide-boom chemical sprayers waste as much as ____ percent of herbicides onto bare ground.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"ninety\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states broadcast spraying 'wastes up to ninety percent of applied chemicals onto non-target weeds and bare soil.'\",\"evidence\":\"broadcast pesticide spraying wastes up to ninety percent of applied chemicals onto non-target weeds and bare soil.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q8\",\"questionNumber\":8,\"prompt\":\"Hyperspectral sensors evaluate crop health by measuring chlorophyll fluorescence and the ____ index.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NDVI\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B refers to 'measuring the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI)'.\",\"evidence\":\"By measuring the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) and specific chlorophyll fluorescence signatures\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q9\",\"questionNumber\":9,\"prompt\":\"Instead of whole-field spraying, advanced weeders deliver micro-droplets directly onto each weed's ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"apical bud\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C mentions 'firing micro-droplets of concentrated organic herbicide directly onto the weed's apical bud'.\",\"evidence\":\"firing micro-droplets of concentrated organic herbicide directly onto the weed's apical bud, or neutralizing it instantly with high-energy carbon dioxide laser pulses.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q10\",\"questionNumber\":10,\"prompt\":\"Some modern robotic weeders eliminate invasive plants using ____ laser pulses instead of chemicals.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"carbon dioxide\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes weed neutralization with 'high-energy carbon dioxide laser pulses.'\",\"evidence\":\"or neutralizing it instantly with high-energy carbon dioxide laser pulses.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q11\",\"questionNumber\":11,\"prompt\":\"Harvesting delicate fruits mechanistically is difficult because human pickers rely on subtle ____ feedback.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"tactile\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states human pickers 'rely on nuanced tactile feedback to pluck ripe fruit without bruising'.\",\"evidence\":\"human pickers rely on nuanced tactile feedback to pluck ripe fruit without bruising tender skin.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q12\",\"questionNumber\":12,\"prompt\":\"Harvesting arms compute fruit positions and stem angles using ____ depth sensors.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"stereoscopic\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D explains arms are 'Guided by stereoscopic depth sensors that compute the exact three-dimensional Cartesian coordinates'.\",\"evidence\":\"Guided by stereoscopic depth sensors that compute the exact three-dimensional Cartesian coordinates and stem angles\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q13\",\"questionNumber\":13,\"prompt\":\"Adopting the ____ business framework enables smaller farms to hire agricultural robots without heavy capital loans.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"Robotics-as-a-Service\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E mentions 'Robotics-as-a-Service (RaaS) subscription models' democratizing access.\",\"evidence\":\"forecast that 'Robotics-as-a-Service' (RaaS) subscription models, wherein third-party robotic operators harvest and weed fields on an hourly fee basis\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":2,\"title\":\"Language Evolution: Dialects, Creoles, and Digital Vernaculars\",\"subtitle\":\"How human languages adapt, branch, and evolve in response to technological and geographic isolation\",\"wordCount\":850,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nLanguage is frequently treated by formal grammarians as a static monument governed by immutable codices of syntax and orthography. In reality, human speech behaves far more like a biological organism—a dynamic, self-organizing adaptive system in a state of perpetual evolutionary flux. Every spoken language is constantly shedding archaic morphological forms, adopting loanwords, reallocating syntactic functions, and generating regional dialects. From the diversification of Proto-Indo-European into modern Hindi, Russian, and Gaelic, to the emergence of novel internet vernaculars on smartphone screens, linguistic evolution reflects humanity's instinct to optimize communicative efficiency while cementing communal identity.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nThe primary catalyst of historical linguistic divergence is geographic and social isolation. When a unified speech community splits and migrates to isolated territories, subtle phonetic drifts occur. Over generations, vowel shifts and consonant mergers accumulate; what began as idiosyncratic regional accents solidify into distinct dialects. If geographic isolation persists for centuries, mutual intelligibility completely breaks down, and what were once dialects cross the evolutionary threshold to become separate sister languages. This divergence is vividly documented in the Romance language family, where regional variants of Vulgar Latin evolved into French, Spanish, Italian, and Romanian following the dissolution of centralized Roman administration.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nA contrasting evolutionary dynamic occurs when disparate populations with mutually unintelligible languages are thrown together in colonial trade hubs, coastal plantations, or borderlands. Under these urgent communicative conditions, speakers improvise a 'pidgin'—a drastically simplified contact code with a reduced lexicon, stripped-down syntax, and zero native speakers. However, the true linguistic miracle occurs when children are born into a pidgin-speaking environment. The innate human grammatical faculty refuses to tolerate the communicative ambiguities of a rudimentary pidgin; within a single generation, these children intuitively flesh out the syntax, invent regular tense markers, and enrich the vocabulary, transmuting the fragile pidgin into a fully formed 'creole' language, capable of expressing poetic nuance and philosophical subtlety.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nIn the twenty-first century, the locus of linguistic evolution has shifted radically from geographic mountain ranges to the borderless expanses of the internet. Digital communication—encompassing messaging applications, gaming servers, and social media platforms—has fostered unprecedented forms of written colloquial speech. Linguistic scholars such as Dr. Gretchen McCulloch identify digital writing not as the degradation of formal literacy, as alarmist cultural critics allege, but as the emergence of a sophisticated new expressive modality known as 'written speech' or 'netspeak'. Because text messages lack physical acoustic tone, posture, and facial expression, digital communicators have innovatively repurposed punctuation marks, emojis, capitalization conventions, and intentional misspellings to convey subtle pragmatic nuances such as irony, enthusiasm, or passive aggression.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nFurthermore, digital algorithms and social platforms have accelerated the velocity of linguistic transmission to unprecedented speeds. In the pre-digital era, a novel slang term or phonetic idiom could take decades to travel from urban counter-cultures into the mainstream lexicon via radio and print journalism. Today, a viral phrase coined by teen content creators on video-sharing platforms can achieve global currency across English-speaking youth demographics within forty-eight hours. While some fear that this rapid globalization will homogenize linguistic diversity, sociolinguists observe the opposite: local communities actively blend global digital idioms with local dialects, generating vibrant hybrid vernaculars that affirm belonging in an interconnected world.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Reading Passage 2 has five paragraphs, A–E. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–E, in boxes 14–19 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"matching_information\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q14\",\"questionNumber\":14,\"prompt\":\"how children spontaneously transform a rudimentary contact jargon into a complete grammar\",\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C describes how children born into a pidgin environment transform it within a single generation into a fully formed creole language.\",\"evidence\":\"within a single generation, these children intuitively flesh out the syntax, invent regular tense markers, and enrich the vocabulary, transmuting the fragile pidgin into a fully formed 'creole' language\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q15\",\"questionNumber\":15,\"prompt\":\"a comparison of language to a living biological organism rather than a fixed monument\",\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A explicitly notes that human speech behaves 'far more like a biological organism—a dynamic, self-organizing adaptive system'.\",\"evidence\":\"In reality, human speech behaves far more like a biological organism—a dynamic, self-organizing adaptive system in a state of perpetual evolutionary flux.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q16\",\"questionNumber\":16,\"prompt\":\"the historical fragmentation of Vulgar Latin into separate modern European tongues\",\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B cites the Romance language family where 'regional variants of Vulgar Latin evolved into French, Spanish, Italian, and Romanian'.\",\"evidence\":\"This divergence is vividly documented in the Romance language family, where regional variants of Vulgar Latin evolved into French, Spanish, Italian, and Romanian\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q17\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"prompt\":\"how digital writers compensate for the absence of vocal inflections and physical gestures\",\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D explains that digital communicators repurpose punctuation, emojis, and capitalization to convey pragmatic nuance.\",\"evidence\":\"Because text messages lack physical acoustic tone, posture, and facial expression, digital communicators have innovatively repurposed punctuation marks, emojis, capitalization conventions\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q18\",\"questionNumber\":18,\"prompt\":\"the dramatically shortened timeframe for new slang to achieve worldwide recognition\",\"correctAnswer\":\"E\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E highlights that viral phrases can now achieve global currency 'within forty-eight hours' compared to decades previously.\",\"evidence\":\"Today, a viral phrase coined by teen content creators on video-sharing platforms can achieve global currency across English-speaking youth demographics within forty-eight hours.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q19\",\"questionNumber\":19,\"prompt\":\"the argument that online writing represents a new communicative modality rather than literacy decay\",\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D notes linguists like Dr. McCulloch view digital writing 'not as the degradation of formal literacy... but as the emergence of a sophisticated new expressive modality'.\",\"evidence\":\"identify digital writing not as the degradation of formal literacy, as alarmist cultural critics allege, but as the emergence of a sophisticated new expressive modality known as 'written speech' or 'netspeak'.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 20–23 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"sentence_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q20\",\"questionNumber\":20,\"prompt\":\"When speech communities remain geographically isolated, distinct regional accents eventually develop into ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"dialects\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that regional accents 'solidify into distinct dialects.'\",\"evidence\":\"Over generations, vowel shifts and consonant mergers accumulate; what began as idiosyncratic regional accents solidify into distinct dialects.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q21\",\"questionNumber\":21,\"prompt\":\"A rudimentary contact system developed for basic trading with no native speakers is known as a ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"pidgin\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C defines a pidgin as 'a drastically simplified contact code with a reduced lexicon, stripped-down syntax, and zero native speakers.'\",\"evidence\":\"speakers improvise a 'pidgin'—a drastically simplified contact code with a reduced lexicon, stripped-down syntax, and zero native speakers.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q22\",\"questionNumber\":22,\"prompt\":\"Unlike pidgins, a fully developed ____ contains complex grammatical structures and can express poetic ideas.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"creole\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C explains how a pidgin becomes a 'fully formed creole language, capable of expressing poetic nuance'.\",\"evidence\":\"transmuting the fragile pidgin into a fully formed 'creole' language, capable of expressing poetic nuance and philosophical subtlety.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q23\",\"questionNumber\":23,\"prompt\":\"Linguist Dr. Gretchen McCulloch describes the hybrid style of online writing as ____ or netspeak.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"written speech\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D refers to the modality known as 'written speech' or 'netspeak'.\",\"evidence\":\"a sophisticated new expressive modality known as 'written speech' or 'netspeak'.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C, or D. Write the correct letter in boxes 24–26 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"multiple_choice\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q24\",\"questionNumber\":24,\"prompt\":\"What marks the transition of regional dialects into independent languages?\",\"options\":[\"The publication of an official school dictionary\",\"The complete breakdown of mutual intelligibility between speakers\",\"The introduction of alphabetic script\",\"A royal decree by the reigning political monarch\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states 'If geographic isolation persists for centuries, mutual intelligibility completely breaks down, and what were once dialects cross the evolutionary threshold to become separate sister languages.'\",\"evidence\":\"If geographic isolation persists for centuries, mutual intelligibility completely breaks down, and what were once dialects cross the evolutionary threshold to become separate sister languages.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q25\",\"questionNumber\":25,\"prompt\":\"Why did children of pidgin speakers develop creoles?\",\"options\":[\"They were punished by teachers for using improper words\",\"They preferred foreign loanwords over local expressions\",\"Their innate grammatical capacity could not tolerate ambiguous communication\",\"They had access to printed grammar textbooks\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C explains 'The innate human grammatical faculty refuses to tolerate the communicative ambiguities of a rudimentary pidgin'.\",\"evidence\":\"The innate human grammatical faculty refuses to tolerate the communicative ambiguities of a rudimentary pidgin; within a single generation, these children intuitively flesh out the syntax\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q26\",\"questionNumber\":26,\"prompt\":\"What is the consequence of digital slang spreading rapidly across the globe?\",\"options\":[\"Complete destruction of all national languages within a decade\",\"A return to nineteenth-century letter-writing norms\",\"The abandonment of smartphones by young communicators\",\"Local communities creating dynamic hybrid blends of global and local vernaculars\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E concludes that 'local communities actively blend global digital idioms with local dialects, generating vibrant hybrid vernaculars'.\",\"evidence\":\"local communities actively blend global digital idioms with local dialects, generating vibrant hybrid vernaculars that affirm belonging in an interconnected world.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":3,\"title\":\"Glaciology and Paleoclimatic Ice Core Archives\",\"subtitle\":\"Drilling miles into polar ice sheets to extract atmospheric time capsules spanning eight hundred thousand years\",\"wordCount\":890,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nBeneath the immense continental ice sheets of Antarctica and Greenland lies an unbroken paleoclimatic chronicle of Earth's atmospheric history. Glaciologists regard polar ice sheets as colossal frozen archives. Each year, falling snow captures ambient atmospheric aerosols, volcanic dust, sea salt, and pollen grains. As successive annual snow layers accumulate under their own mounting weight, the porous surface snow (firn) compresses into solid, impermeable glacial ice. Crucially, during this densification process, minute bubbles of ambient air are hermetically sealed within the crystalline ice matrix. These encapsulated air bubbles represent pristine physical samples of Earth's ancient paleo-atmosphere, preserved without chemical alteration for hundreds of thousands of years.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nExtracting these fragile atmospheric records requires extraordinary engineering feats in sub-zero polar environments. Using electromechanical and thermal rotary drill heads suspended on armored cables, glaciological teams drill deep boreholes to depths exceeding three kilometers. At the EPICA (European Project for Ice Coring in Antarctica) site at Dome Concordia—a remote polar plateau where surface temperatures plunge below minus eighty degrees Celsius—scientists successfully recovered a continuous ice cylinder 3,270 meters long, capturing a climatic chronicle spanning 800,000 continuous years. Core segments must be extracted with extreme care; deep ice cores under enormous lithostatic pressure can shatter catastrophically upon retrieval if subjected to sudden thermal shocks.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nOnce brought to specialized refrigerated laboratories, ice cores undergo rigorous mass-spectrometric and isotopic analysis. Glaciologists determine historical air temperatures by examining stable isotope ratios of water molecules, specifically the ratio of heavy oxygen-18 to light oxygen-16 (denoted as delta-O-18), as well as deuterium (hydrogen-2) to regular hydrogen-1. Water molecules bearing heavier isotopes condense and precipitate more readily than lighter isotopes as moisture masses migrate from tropical latitudes toward the frigid poles. Consequently, during colder global glacial epochs, snowfall reaching the polar ice sheets contains significantly depleted concentrations of oxygen-18. By measuring isotopic fluctuations down the core with sub-annual precision, paleoclimatologists reconstruct ancient surface temperature regimes with astonishing fidelity.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nSimultaneously, the trapped air bubbles are extracted using mechanical vacuum crushers and melted under inert gas environments to measure greenhouse gas concentrations. The historical data yielded by these gas extractions is unequivocal: throughout the past eight glacial-interglacial cycles, atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations oscillated reliably between 180 parts per million (ppm) during deep ice ages and roughly 280 ppm during warm interglacial periods. Never in the 800,000-year ice core record did carbon dioxide levels exceed 300 ppm. In stark contrast, contemporary atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations have surpassed 420 ppm due to post-industrial fossil fuel combustion. Ice cores provide empirical proof that current greenhouse gas levels are completely unprecedented in human evolutionary history.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nFurthermore, ice core archives document the dramatic reality of abrupt climatic shifts, known as Dansgaard-Oeschger (D-O) events. Greenland ice core records revealed that during the last glacial cycle, localized temperatures repeatedly spiked by as much as 10 to 15 degrees Celsius within just a few decades—a geologically instantaneous timescale. Glaciologists discovered that these sudden warmings were triggered by rapid reorganizations of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), the ocean conveyor current that distributes tropical warmth to high latitudes. When freshwater influx from melting glaciers disrupts the density-driven sinking of cold, salty water in the North Atlantic, the entire ocean conveyor abruptly stutters or collapses, plunging regions into severe climatic instability. By proving that the planetary climate system can tip abruptly between discrete equilibrium states, ice core science provides an indispensable warning for contemporary climate policy.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer in Reading Passage 3? In boxes 27–31 on your answer sheet, write:\\nYES if the statement agrees with the views of the writer\\nNO if the statement contradicts the views of the writer\\nNOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this\",\"questionType\":\"yes_no_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q27\",\"questionNumber\":27,\"prompt\":\"Air bubbles trapped in polar ice provide unaltered physical samples of historical atmospheres.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"YES\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states air bubbles 'represent pristine physical samples of Earth's ancient paleo-atmosphere, preserved without chemical alteration'.\",\"evidence\":\"These encapsulated air bubbles represent pristine physical samples of Earth's ancient paleo-atmosphere, preserved without chemical alteration for hundreds of thousands of years.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q28\",\"questionNumber\":28,\"prompt\":\"Ice cores retrieved from extreme depths are resistant to damage from temperature changes.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NO\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explicitly warns that deep ice cores 'can shatter catastrophically upon retrieval if subjected to sudden thermal shocks.'\",\"evidence\":\"deep ice cores under enormous lithostatic pressure can shatter catastrophically upon retrieval if subjected to sudden thermal shocks.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q29\",\"questionNumber\":29,\"prompt\":\"During colder glacial eras, polar snowfall shows a depletion in heavy oxygen-18 isotopes.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"YES\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes that 'during colder global glacial epochs, snowfall reaching the polar ice sheets contains significantly depleted concentrations of oxygen-18.'\",\"evidence\":\"Consequently, during colder global glacial epochs, snowfall reaching the polar ice sheets contains significantly depleted concentrations of oxygen-18.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q30\",\"questionNumber\":30,\"prompt\":\"Prior to industrialization, carbon dioxide concentrations frequently exceeded 350 parts per million.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NO\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states: 'Never in the 800,000-year ice core record did carbon dioxide levels exceed 300 ppm.'\",\"evidence\":\"Never in the 800,000-year ice core record did carbon dioxide levels exceed 300 ppm. In stark contrast, contemporary atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations have surpassed 420 ppm\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q31\",\"questionNumber\":31,\"prompt\":\"The EPICA drilling project cost more than one hundred million euros to complete.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B discusses the EPICA project at Dome Concordia, but nowhere in the text is the project's financial cost mentioned.\",\"evidence\":\"At the EPICA (European Project for Ice Coring in Antarctica) site at Dome Concordia—a remote polar plateau where surface temperatures plunge below minus eighty degrees Celsius\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Reading Passage 3 has five paragraphs, A–E. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–E, in boxes 32–36 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"matching_information\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q32\",\"questionNumber\":32,\"prompt\":\"a reference to sudden massive regional temperature spikes within a few decades\",\"correctAnswer\":\"E\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E refers to Dansgaard-Oeschger events where 'temperatures repeatedly spiked by as much as 10 to 15 degrees Celsius within just a few decades'.\",\"evidence\":\"localized temperatures repeatedly spiked by as much as 10 to 15 degrees Celsius within just a few decades—a geologically instantaneous timescale.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q33\",\"questionNumber\":33,\"prompt\":\"the conversion of porous surface snow into impermeable glacial ice\",\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A describes how porous surface snow (firn) compresses into solid, impermeable glacial ice.\",\"evidence\":\"As successive annual snow layers accumulate under their own mounting weight, the porous surface snow (firn) compresses into solid, impermeable glacial ice.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q34\",\"questionNumber\":34,\"prompt\":\"the historical boundary range of carbon dioxide levels between cold and warm eras\",\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D details carbon dioxide oscillating 'between 180 parts per million (ppm) during deep ice ages and roughly 280 ppm during warm interglacial periods.'\",\"evidence\":\"atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations oscillated reliably between 180 parts per million (ppm) during deep ice ages and roughly 280 ppm during warm interglacial periods.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q35\",\"questionNumber\":35,\"prompt\":\"the extraction of a three-kilometer ice cylinder covering eight hundred thousand years\",\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B describes drilling at EPICA to recover 'a continuous ice cylinder 3,270 meters long, capturing a climatic chronicle spanning 800,000 continuous years.'\",\"evidence\":\"scientists successfully recovered a continuous ice cylinder 3,270 meters long, capturing a climatic chronicle spanning 800,000 continuous years.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q36\",\"questionNumber\":36,\"prompt\":\"the use of isotopic ratios of oxygen and hydrogen to deduce past temperatures\",\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C describes examining ratios of oxygen-18 to oxygen-16 and deuterium to hydrogen-1 to reconstruct surface temperature regimes.\",\"evidence\":\"Glaciologists determine historical air temperatures by examining stable isotope ratios of water molecules, specifically the ratio of heavy oxygen-18 to light oxygen-16\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 37–40 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a4-q37\",\"questionNumber\":37,\"prompt\":\"The scientific term for granular, porous surface snow that has not yet compacted into ice is ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"firn\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A identifies porous surface snow as 'firn'.\",\"evidence\":\"the porous surface snow (firn) compresses into solid, impermeable glacial ice.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q38\",\"questionNumber\":38,\"prompt\":\"During deep ice ages over the last 800,000 years, atmospheric carbon dioxide fell as low as ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"180 ppm\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D notes CO2 levels reached '180 parts per million (ppm) during deep ice ages'.\",\"evidence\":\"atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations oscillated reliably between 180 parts per million (ppm) during deep ice ages\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q39\",\"questionNumber\":39,\"prompt\":\"Abrupt paleoclimatic temperature jumps identified in Greenland ice are scientifically designated as ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"Dansgaard-Oeschger events\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E refers to 'abrupt climatic shifts, known as Dansgaard-Oeschger (D-O) events.'\",\"evidence\":\"Furthermore, ice core archives document the dramatic reality of abrupt climatic shifts, known as Dansgaard-Oeschger (D-O) events.\"},{\"id\":\"a4-q40\",\"questionNumber\":40,\"prompt\":\"Rapid warming spikes occurred when freshwater influx disrupted the oceanic current known as the ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"AMOC\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E names the 'Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC)'.\",\"evidence\":\"triggered by rapid reorganizations of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), the ocean conveyor current\"}]}]}]");
